   “Let us count to eight,” said Vale. “Together.”



He led it himself.
   “One,” said Vale, softly, into the microphone, “two.”
   And the room said it back.
   Not loudly. Not like a pledge. Like children learning, a murmur
from eleven hundred throats, with the gentle obedience of people
who’d been asked kindly. Three. Four. I watched it travel across the car‐
pet in a wave. Five. Six. The donors, the choir mothers, the ushers. Sev‐
en. Doña Fela’s lips were moving. Don Chino’s wife had closed her
eyes. Eight.
   The pause.
   Then again. One.
   Eleven hundred people had stopped listening to the tone and started
making it. Eight seconds around, in their own mouths, until their
breathing fell into it. A loop made of people.
   The Garden went gray.
